 "findings": [
  {
   "id": "V4-F1",
   "severity": "blocking",
   "check": "1 cost accounting / section 5a",
   "finding": "rhodp's per-step native work is neither counted nor emitted: one canonicalisation per computed point (y <= (p-1)/2, conditional negation), two step-index hashes and one DP test per step. Over its 2376 runs the instrumented replay counts 7266086859 canonicalisations, 14018374537 step-index hashes and 7009187296 DP tests against H = 766313717 priced hash operations (37-5886 canonicalisations per target). AGENTS.md 5a names canonicalisations and partition hashes as rho's unpriced work that every ranking must list with counts and price at 0/0.1/1 GAE, and ecbench prices prime-field rho's canonicalisations_uncharged in the band (src/cryptanalysis/ecbench/band.rs). The frozen rule prices h on H + L only, so rhodp, the h = 1 reference, is free of that work while ic5 pays h for every table probe. With rhodp's canonicalisations priced at h (P1), icbest vs rhobest becomes {'unranked': 27}: at I = 10 M the IC is 0.888-0.935x the cheapest rho at h = 1 (cheaper). Pricing also the step-index hashes and DP tests (P2): {'unranked': 27}. Against the validator's stronger references (R2: rho2xks at kappa 0.05-0.25 and rhodpu, below) P1 gives {'unranked': 20, 'dearer': 7}. The 27 'dearer' decisions are decisions of the frozen rule, not section-5a decisions."
  },
  {
   "id": "V4-F2",
   "severity": "blocking",
   "check": "1 fairness of ic5ks (is the IC side handicapped?)",
   "finding": "ic5ks's retention is degenerate. Its trials use one shared arithmetic progression W_k = W_0 + k D, so a retained class x(Q_t +- W_k) meets a later trial x(Q' +- W_k') only if Q' -+ Q_t lies in {j D} or {+-(2 W_0 + j D)}: O(K) points per solved target instead of O(K K'). Exact count (validation/scripts/coverage.py, K = K' = 1000): 11994 distinct solvable target logs with the progression against 3292090 with a non-progression W. This, not a fixed IC table, is why retention gains ic5 at most 1.5% (RESULTS 'Why' attributes it to the table being fixed after phase 1). Validator arm ic5rw (validation/scripts/patch_ic5rw.py: ic5ks with W_(k+1) = W_k + D_(j(k)) over 32 random known-log points, 32 counted scalar multiplications; nothing else changed; not pre-registered) at mu 1.0/0.85/0.7/0.6/0.5 (990 runs, every target verified by the harness): the best ic5rw is 0.791-0.928x (median 0.834) of icbest at I = 10 M, h = 0. The control ic5w (same W, no retention) is 1.001x ic5 (median), so the gain is retention. With ic5rw in the candidate set: vs the frozen rhobest {'unranked': 27} (frozen rule; IC cheaper at h = 0, 0.897-0.960 at I = 10 M); vs R1 (rho2xks also tuned down) {'unranked': 13, 'dearer': 14}; vs R2 {'unranked': 13, 'dearer': 14}; under P1 vs R2 {'unranked': 19, 'cheaper': 6, 'dearer': 2}. mu = 0.5 (grid floor) was best in most configurations, so the IC side is not fully tuned either."
  },
  {
   "id": "V4-F3",
   "severity": "major",
   "check": "5 wording / reference",
   "finding": "No single rho arm beats the IC across the band: icbest vs rho2xks alone is {'unranked': 27} (IC cheaper at h = 1, 0.875-0.914 at I = 10 M) and vs rhodp alone {'unranked': 27} (IC cheaper at h = 0, 0.711-0.853). The 'dearer' verdict is against the per-price envelope of two different rho algorithms (rho2xks at h = 0 and 0.1, rhodp at h = 1). That envelope is a legitimate reference (an attacker picks the rho for its hash price) but it must be stated; analysis.txt shows it, RESULTS does not."
  },
  {
   "id": "V4-F4",
   "severity": "major (direction: favours the IC)",
   "check": "1 reference strength",
   "finding": "Both rho arms are under-tuned or handicapped, which makes the frozen-rule result conservative. (a) rho2xks's frozen kappa grid {0.5, 0.7, 1.0} has its best at the floor (0.5 in all 198); the validator's kappa 0.05/0.1/0.25 (594 runs) is cheaper still ({'rho2xks_lo 0.05': 125, 'rho2xks_lo 0.1': 70, 'rho2xks_lo 0.25': 3} at I = 10 M, h = 0), raising icbest/rho at h = 0 to 1.094-1.280. (b) rhodp draws its 1024 steps as B_j + B_k with j, k uniform over 64 bank points, so about 39% of steps share their value with another index; a sign flip followed by such an index is a fruitless 2-cycle that the same-index look-ahead does not catch, and the walk is abandoned after 20 * 2^d counted steps: 1-34% of rhodp's steps are spent in abandoned walks (5-20% at 32 bits for the offsets that win at h = 1). Validator arm rhodpu (distinct step pairs, nothing else changed; 2376 runs) cuts abandoned walks to 4.5e-05 per walk and its best h = 1 cost to 0.809-0.993x (median 0.924) of rhodp's. Frozen rule against R2: {'dearer': 27}, I = 10 M medians h = 0 1.094-1.280, h = 1 1.215-2.035. rhodp is otherwise a correct multi-target DP rho: negation-map walk on canonical classes (a deterministic function of the class, the look-ahead included), DP database, own-walk opposite-sign collisions solved as q = (a2 - a)/(2b), Kuhn-Struik retention of the pending DPs after the solve, optional known-log precomputation; every addition, lookup and insert is counted."
  },
  {
   "id": "V4-F5",
   "severity": "info",
   "check": "1 free information / retention correctness",
   "finding": "No free information found. The planted logs are used only for planting (uncounted copy of the field) and in the final check; the new arms derive every log from known-log bank points and the recovered log. Every retained point's claimed log was checked on a sample from every run: 693769 logs ({'icret': 59400, 'ks': 177651, 'dpret': 283709, 'dppre': 173009}), 0 failures. HashMap calls equal H in every run of ic5, rho2xks, rhodp and bsgs2. ic5ks performs two lookups per trial class (retained map, then table) and counts one: 1173632323 calls against H = 888236477; one merged table whose retained entries overwrite table entries gives the same answers with one probe, so the count is defensible; it favours the candidate. Abandoned rhodp walks keep their counted steps; rhodp's reported target_steps omits each target's solving walk (a report field only; costs come from the field counters)."
  },
